.TH KP-MANAGER 1 "October 2026" "kp-manager 2.0.0" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
kp\-manager \- hardened backup and sync manager for KeePass (.kdbx) databases on a PC, USB flash drive, NAS and Google Drive
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B kp\-manager
[\fIOPTIONS\fR]
.I COMMAND
[\fIARGUMENTS\fR]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B kp\-manager
keeps copies of one KeePass database (a \fI.kdbx\fR file) in four places: the \fBPC\fR, a
\fBUSB flash drive\fR, a \fBNAS\fR share (CIFS/SMB) and \fBGoogle Drive\fR (through \fBrclone\fR).
.PP
The copy on the PC is the \fIcurrent version\fR and the source of every update. When a location is
updated, the version that was there is first saved in that location's backups folder as
.IR DATABASE _ YYYYMMDD _ HHMMSS . EXT
(for example \fIDatabase_20261011_143000.kdbx\fR) and only then replaced.
Nothing is ever overwritten without a verified copy of the old version.
.PP
.B kp\-manager
never opens or decrypts the database. It copies, compares, checks and renames files only.
.SH SAFETY GUARANTEES
.TP
.B One instance at a time
Every command that writes takes an exclusive \fBflock\fR(1) lock on
\fI~/.local/state/kp\-manager/kp\-manager.lock\fR. A second instance exits at once with status 75 instead of
waiting, so two runs can never write to the database or sync at the same time.
.TP
.B SHA\-256 verification
Every copy is verified three times: the source is hashed before the copy, the temporary file is hashed
after it is written, and the final file is hashed again after it has been moved into place.
Any mismatch aborts the operation. Google Drive provides no SHA\-256, so files there are downloaded
and hashed locally rather than trusting MD5.
.TP
.B Atomic replacement
A file is first written under a hidden temporary name next to its destination
(\fI.NAME.kp\-tmp.PID\fR), verified, and only then renamed over the destination. An interrupted or failed
copy therefore never leaves a half-written database; the old file stays intact.
.TP
.B Cleanup on exit
A trap on EXIT, INT (Ctrl+C), TERM and HUP removes temporary files, unmounts a NAS or USB drive that
\fBkp\-manager\fR mounted itself, removes its scratch directory and releases the lock, even after a crash
or interruption. A share that was already mounted before the run is left mounted.
.TP
.B Sanity checks
Before a database is copied, \fBkp\-manager\fR checks that it starts with the KeePass 2.x file signature,
that it is not less than half the size of the copy it would replace, and that the copy on the target is
not newer than the one on the PC. A failed check is a \fIsafety warning\fR: it stops the run unless you
answer yes at the prompt or pass \fB\-\-force\fR. Without a terminal the run is refused.
.TP
.B Private files
Files are created with mode 600. The config file must be owned by you and not writable by group or others;
the NAS credentials file must not be accessible by others. Otherwise \fBkp\-manager\fR refuses to use it.
.SH COMMANDS
Locations are \fBpc\fR, \fBflash\fR, \fBnas\fR and \fBgdrive\fR. Aliases: \fBusb\fR and \fBstick\fR (flash);
\fBgd\fR, \fBgoogle\fR (gdrive); \fBlocal\fR (pc).
.TP
\fBinit\fR
Interactive setup wizard. Asks for every default path and setting (Enter keeps the value shown in
brackets, \fB\-\fR clears it), offers to store the NAS login in a protected file
(\fI~/.config/kp\-manager/nas.cred\fR, mode 600), saves the config file, and then tests the connections:
PC database, backup folder, USB drives, NAS (a real mount and unmount) and the rclone remote.
The wizard also starts by itself on the first run of any command when no config file exists.
.TP
\fBupdate\-flash\fR
Scans for USB partitions, shows a numbered list and lets you choose one (\fBq\fR cancels). The partition
is mounted if necessary. If the flash drive already has a database it is checked, backed up with a
timestamp in the drive's backups folder, and then the PC database is copied over it with full
verification. Afterwards the drive is unmounted again if \fBkp\-manager\fR mounted it.
Encrypted (LUKS), swap and RAID members and system mount points are never offered.
.TP
\fBupdate\-nas\fR
Mounts the share with \fBsudo mount \-t cifs\fR using your credentials file (or \fBNAS_USER\fR; the
password is then asked by mount), backs up the NAS database with a timestamp, writes the PC database,
verifies it, and cleanly unmounts. If the share is already mounted it is used and left mounted.
Without \fBNAS_MOUNT\fR a temporary mount point is created and removed again.
.TP
\fBupdate\-gdrive\fR
Same procedure through \fBrclone\fR. The old remote database is copied into the remote backups folder first
(rotation is controlled by \fBKEEP_BACKUPS\fR), and every rclone call is retried with exponential
back-off, which copes with Drive rate limits (HTTP 429, quota messages). The uploaded file is read back
and its SHA\-256 compared with the source.
.TP
\fBupdate\-all\fR
Runs flash, NAS and Google Drive one after another. A failure in one does not stop the others; the
exit status is 1 if any failed.
.TP
\fBbackup\-pc\fR
Copies the PC database into the PC backups folder as \fIDATABASE_YYYYMMDD_HHMMSS.EXT\fR.
.TP
\fBbackup\-flash\fR, \fBbackup\-nas\fR, \fBbackup\-gdrive\fR
Copies the database that is \fIalready on that location\fR into that location's own backups folder.
.TP
\fBrestore\fR [\fIlocation\fR] [\fIname\fR]
Rolls the PC database back. \fIlocation\fR defaults to \fBpc\fR. Without \fIname\fR you choose from a numbered
list of timestamped backups, newest first. \fIname\fR can be a backup file name, \fBlatest\fR (the newest backup)
or \fBcurrent\fR (the live database of flash, nas or gdrive). The selected file is checked and verified, and
the current PC database is backed up first, so a restore can itself be undone.
.TP
\fBlist\fR \fIlocation\fR
Prints the backups at a location, oldest first.
.TP
\fBdiff\fR \fIlocation\fR
Compares checksum, size and modification time of the PC database and the one at the location.
Exit status 0 = identical, 4 = different or missing.
.TP
\fBprune\fR \fIlocation\fR \fB\-\-keep\fR \fIN\fR
Deletes all but the \fIN\fR newest backups at a location (after asking).
.TP
\fBconfig\fR [\fBshow\fR|\fBget\fR \fIKEY\fR|\fBset\fR \fIKEY\fR \fIVALUE\fR|\fBedit\fR|\fBpath\fR]
Shows or changes single settings without running the wizard.
.TP
\fBinfo\fR
Prints settings, file locations, the name the next backup would get and which programs are installed.
.TP
\fBcompletion\fR \fBbash\fR
Prints a bash completion script: \fBsource <(kp\-manager completion bash)\fR.
.TP
\fBhelp\fR, \fBversion\fR
Print the built-in help or the version.
.SH OPTIONS
Options may appear before or after the command. Single-letter flags can be combined (\fB\-nv\fR).
.TP
\fB\-n\fR, \fB\-\-dry\-run\fR
Simulate every copy, move, delete and mount: the exact actions are printed (and logged), nothing is
changed and no drive or share is mounted. Because nothing is mounted, a dry run cannot look inside a
NAS or USB drive that is not mounted yet.
.TP
\fB\-v\fR, \fB\-\-verbose\fR
Print detailed steps, including every SHA\-256 value before the copy, of the temporary file and after the move.
.TP
\fB\-f\fR, \fB\-\-force\fR
Bypass safety warnings: identical hashes (update anyway), a source that is not a KDBX file, a target
that is much larger or newer. Use with care.
.TP
\fB\-y\fR, \fB\-\-yes\fR
Answer routine questions with yes (delete old backups? replace the PC database?) and pick the USB drive
automatically when exactly one is found. It does \fInot\fR override safety warnings; use \fB\-\-force\fR for that.
.TP
\fB\-h\fR, \fB\-\-help\fR
Display the formatted usage text.
.TP
\fB\-c\fR, \fB\-\-config\fR \fIFILE\fR
Use another config file.
.TP
\fB\-\-db\fR \fIFILE\fR
Use \fIFILE\fR as the PC database for this run (overrides \fBDB_PATH\fR).
.TP
\fB\-d\fR, \fB\-\-device\fR \fIDEV\fR
Use this USB partition (for example \fI/dev/sdb1\fR) and skip the menu.
.TP
\fB\-\-usb\-path\fR \fIDIR\fR
Treat \fIDIR\fR as the root of the flash drive. No menu, no mounting.
.TP
\fB\-\-no\-mount\fR
Never mount or unmount anything; the NAS folder and the flash drive must already be available.
.TP
\fB\-k\fR, \fB\-\-keep\fR \fIN\fR
Number of backups to keep (\fBprune\fR; also overrides \fBKEEP_BACKUPS\fR for automatic rotation).
.TP
\fB\-\-no\-color\fR
No colors. Colors are also off when standard error is not a terminal or \fBNO_COLOR\fR is set.
.TP
\fB\-V\fR, \fB\-\-version\fR
Print the version.
.SH CONFIGURATION
Settings live in
.I ~/.config/kp\-manager/config.conf
as \fIKEY="value"\fR lines; lines starting with # are comments. \fB~\fR and \fB$HOME\fR are expanded.
Values must not contain double quotes or line breaks. The file is created with mode 600 by \fBinit\fR.
The database has the same file name everywhere (the base name of \fBDB_PATH\fR). Folders on the flash drive,
NAS and Drive are relative to the drive root, the share root or the rclone remote root and must not
contain \fB..\fR.
.TP
\fBDB_PATH\fR (~/KeePass/Database.kdbx)
The PC database: current version and source of every update.
.TP
\fBPC_BACKUP_DIR\fR (~/KeePass/Backups)
Backups folder on the PC.
.TP
\fBUSB_DB_DIR\fR (KeePass), \fBUSB_BACKUP_DIR\fR (KeePass/Backups)
Database and backups folders on the flash drive.
.TP
\fBNAS_ADDR\fR (//192.168.1.1/KeePass), \fBNAS_MOUNT\fR (/mnt/nas/KeePass)
The CIFS share and where it is mounted. Empty \fBNAS_MOUNT\fR means a temporary mount point per run.
.TP
\fBNAS_USER\fR (nasuser)
User name, used when there is no credentials file; the password is then asked by mount.
.TP
\fBNAS_CREDENTIALS\fR (empty)
Credentials file with \fBusername=\fR and \fBpassword=\fR lines, mode 600 (written by \fBinit\fR). It is passed
to mount as \fBcredentials=FILE\fR, so the password never appears on a command line.
.TP
\fBNAS_MOUNT_OPTS\fR (empty)
Extra mount options, comma separated, for example \fBvers=3.0\fR. Mounts always use
\fBuid\fR, \fBgid\fR, \fBfile_mode=0600\fR, \fBdir_mode=0700\fR, \fBnosuid\fR, \fBnodev\fR and \fBnoexec\fR.
.TP
\fBNAS_DB_DIR\fR (empty), \fBNAS_BACKUP_DIR\fR (Backups)
Database and backups folders inside the share. Empty database folder = share root.
.TP
\fBGDRIVE_REMOTE\fR (gdrive)
Name of the rclone remote that points to Google Drive.
.TP
\fBGDRIVE_DB_DIR\fR (KeePass), \fBGDRIVE_BACKUP_DIR\fR (KeePass/Backups)
Database and backups folders on Google Drive.
.TP
\fBGDRIVE_RETRIES\fR (4)
Attempts per rclone call, 1 to 10. The wait starts at 2 seconds and doubles each time.
.TP
\fBKEEP_BACKUPS\fR (0)
After each successful update or backup keep only the newest N backups at that location (0 = keep all).
Also the default for \fBprune \-\-keep\fR.
.SH LOCATIONS IN DETAIL
.SS USB flash drive
Drives are found with \fBlsblk\fR: removable or hot-pluggable partitions that carry a file system.
Mounting uses \fBudisksctl\fR (no root needed); without udisks2, \fBsudo mount\fR is used on
\fI/mnt/kp\-manager\-usb\fR, where file systems that store Unix permissions (such as ext4) may end up owned
by root. FAT, exFAT and NTFS are mounted with your uid and gid.
.SS NAS
Needs \fBcifs\-utils\fR (\fBmount.cifs\fR) and, unless you are root, \fBsudo\fR.
.SS Google Drive
Needs \fBrclone\fR with a remote of type \fBdrive\fR. Create it once with \fBrclone config\fR and name it as
\fBGDRIVE_REMOTE\fR; the wizard tests it. rclone keeps and refreshes the login token itself.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I ~/.config/kp\-manager/config.conf
Settings (mode 600).
.TP
.I ~/.config/kp\-manager/nas.cred
NAS login written by \fBinit\fR (mode 600).
.TP
.I ~/.local/state/kp\-manager/activity.log
Every operation with a timestamp and a level (INFO, STEP, SUCCESS, WARN, ERROR, DRYRUN, DEBUG), including
the SHA\-256 of every copy. Rotated to \fIactivity.log.1\fR at 1 MiB.
.TP
.I ~/.local/state/kp\-manager/kp\-manager.lock
The lock file.
.TP
.I *_BACKUP_DIR/DATABASE_YYYYMMDD_HHMMSS.EXT
Backups. If a name is already taken (two runs within one second) \fB_2\fR, \fB_3\fR, ... is appended.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B KP_MANAGER_CONFIG
Config file to use instead of the default.
.TP
.B KP_MANAGER_RETRY_DELAY
First retry delay in seconds for rclone (default 2).
.TP
.B VISUAL, EDITOR
Editor for \fBconfig edit\fR.
.TP
.B NO_COLOR
Disable colors.
.TP
.B XDG_RUNTIME_DIR, TMPDIR
Where temporary mount points and scratch files are created.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Success.
.TP
.B 1
An error occurred, or at least one location failed.
.TP
.B 2
Wrong usage (unknown command, option or location).
.TP
.B 4
\fBdiff\fR only: the databases differ, or the location has none.
.TP
.B 75
Another \fBkp\-manager\fR is running (lock held).
.TP
.B 129, 130, 143
Interrupted by HUP, INT (Ctrl+C) or TERM; cleanup has run.
.SH EXAMPLES
First-time setup, then a safety copy on the PC:
.PP
.RS
.nf
kp\-manager init
kp\-manager backup\-pc
.fi
.RE
.PP
Update the flash drive (choose it from a numbered list), see what an NAS update would do, then do it:
.PP
.RS
.nf
kp\-manager update\-flash
kp\-manager \-\-dry\-run update\-nas
kp\-manager \-\-verbose update\-nas
.fi
.RE
.PP
Update everything and check that the NAS copy matches:
.PP
.RS
.nf
kp\-manager update\-all
kp\-manager diff nas
.fi
.RE
.PP
Roll back: pick a PC backup from a list, or take the newest NAS backup:
.PP
.RS
.nf
kp\-manager restore
kp\-manager restore nas latest
.fi
.RE
.PP
Keep only the 20 newest Google Drive backups:
.PP
.RS
.nf
kp\-manager prune gdrive \-\-keep 20
.fi
.RE
.SH DEPENDENCIES
\fBbash\fR 4.4 or newer and GNU coreutils, findutils and util\-linux (\fBsha256sum\fR, \fBflock\fR, \fBlsblk\fR,
\fBmountpoint\fR). Checked at start\-up for the command being run: \fBrclone\fR for Google Drive,
\fBmount.cifs\fR (package cifs\-utils) and \fBsudo\fR for the NAS, \fBlsblk\fR for the USB menu.
A missing program is reported together with the package to install.
.SH NOTES
Save and close the database in KeePass before updating so that the file on disk is the version you mean to copy.
.PP
Checksums are of the encrypted file; a match means the copy is byte for byte the same file.
.PP
If a key file is part of your master key, \fBkp\-manager\fR does not copy it; keep it elsewhere on purpose.
.SH SEE ALSO
\fBrclone\fR(1), \fBflock\fR(1), \fBmount.cifs\fR(8), \fBudisksctl\fR(1), \fBlsblk\fR(8), \fBsha256sum\fR(1)
